# Formal Grammar & Concrete Syntax
File: openOODA/docs/spec/02_grammar.oot

This module defines the normative Extended Backus-Naur Form (EBNF) grammar,
syntactic invariants, statement forms, and operator precedence of openOODA.

## 1. Top-Level Module Grammar

A source file consists of top-level items:
```ebnf
SourceFile   ::= { TopLevelItem } ;
TopLevelItem ::= ImportDecl | FunctionDecl | TypeDecl | VerifyBlock ;
```

### 1.1 Import Declarations
Imports use flat relative string literals. Double-colon (::) is prohibited:
```ebnf
ImportDecl ::= "import" StringLiteral ";" ;
```

### 1.2 Function Declarations
Functions declare parameter lists, optional return types, and contracts:
```ebnf
FunctionDecl    ::= [ "pub" ] [ "export" ] "fn" Identifier
                    "(" [ ParamList ] ")" [ "->" Type ]
                    [ ContractList ] Block ;
ParamList       ::= Param { "," Param } ;
Param           ::= [ "mut" ] Identifier ":" Type ;
ContractList    ::= { RequiresClause | EnsuresClause } ;
RequiresClause  ::= "requires" Expression ;
EnsuresClause   ::= "ensures" Expression ;
```

### 1.3 Type Declarations
Types define structs or tagged unions (algebraic sum types):
```ebnf
TypeDecl        ::= [ "pub" ] "type" Identifier "=" TypeDef ";" ;
TypeDef         ::= StructDef | SumTypeDef | AliasDef ;
StructDef       ::= "struct" "{" StructFieldList "}" ;
StructFieldList ::= [ StructField { "," StructField } ] ;
StructField     ::= Identifier ":" Type ;
SumTypeDef      ::= VariantDef { "|" VariantDef } ;
VariantDef      ::= Identifier [ "(" TypeList ")" ] ;
TypeList        ::= Type { "," Type } ;
```

### 1.4 Verify Blocks
Verify blocks specify co-located unit verification tests:
```ebnf
VerifyBlock     ::= "verify" Identifier Block ;
```

## 2. Syntactic Invariants

### 2.1 Strictly No Parentheses Around Condition Blocks
Condition expressions in if and while constructs forbid outer parentheses:
- Valid: `if x > 0 { ... }`
- Valid: `while running { ... }`
- Invalid: `if (x > 0) { ... }` (Triggers ERR\tparse)
- Invalid: `while (running) { ... }` (Triggers ERR\tparse)

### 2.2 Zero Struct Trailing Commas
Struct type definitions and struct literals forbid a trailing comma:
- Valid: `type Point = struct { x: Int, y: Int }`
- Valid: `let p = Point { x: 10, y: 20 };`
- Invalid: `type Point = struct { x: Int, y: Int, }` (Triggers ERR\tparse)
- Invalid: `let p = Point { x: 10, y: 20, };` (Triggers ERR\tparse)

## 3. Statements & Blocks

```ebnf
Block       ::= "{" { Statement } "}" ;
Statement   ::= LetStmt
              | AssignStmt
              | IfStmt
              | WhileStmt
              | ForStmt
              | MatchStmt
              | ReturnStmt
              | BreakStmt
              | ContinueStmt
              | ExprStmt ;

LetStmt      ::= "let" [ "mut" ] Identifier [ ":" Type ]
                 [ "=" Expression ] ";" ;
AssignStmt   ::= TargetExpr "=" Expression ";" ;
ExprStmt     ::= Expression ";" ;
ReturnStmt   ::= "return" [ Expression ] ";" ;
BreakStmt    ::= "break" ";" ;
ContinueStmt ::= "continue" ";" ;
```

### 3.1 Control Flow Statements
```ebnf
IfStmt    ::= "if" Expression Block [ "else" ( IfStmt | Block ) ] ;
WhileStmt ::= "while" Expression Block ;
ForStmt   ::= "for" Identifier "in" Expression Block ;
```

### 3.2 Match Statement & Pattern Arms
```ebnf
MatchStmt ::= "match" Expression "{" { MatchArm } "}" ;
MatchArm  ::= Pattern "=>" ( Block | Expression [ "," ] ) ;
Pattern   ::= LiteralPattern
            | IdentPattern
            | VariantPattern
            | WildcardPattern ;
WildcardPattern ::= "_" ;
VariantPattern  ::= Identifier [ "(" PatternList ")" ] ;
PatternList     ::= Pattern { "," Pattern } ;
```

## 4. Expressions & Operator Precedence

Operators evaluate according to the following precedence hierarchy (1 to 13),
matching parser semantics in ast/parse_expr.oo:

| Level | Operator Kind     | Operators         | Associativity |
|-------|-------------------|-------------------|---------------|
| 1     | Assignment        | =                 | Right         |
| 2     | Logical OR        | ||                | Left          |
| 3     | Logical AND       | &&                | Left          |
| 4     | Bitwise OR        | |                 | Left          |
| 5     | Bitwise XOR       | ^                 | Left          |
| 6     | Bitwise AND       | &                 | Left          |
| 7     | Equality          | ==, !=            | Left          |
| 8     | Relational        | <, <=, >, >=      | Left          |
| 9     | Bit Shift         | <<, >>            | Left          |
| 10    | Additive          | +, -              | Left          |
| 11    | Multiplicative    | *, /, %           | Left          |
| 12    | Unary / Prefix    | !, -, &, &mut, *  | Right         |
| 13    | Primary / Postfix | ., [], (), ?      | Left          |

Precedence example:
- `1 << 1 + 1 == 4` evaluates as `1 << (1 + 1) == 4` (equals `true`).
- `true || false && false == true` evaluates as `true || (false && false)`.
- `3 | 4 ^ 5 == 3` evaluates as `3 | (4 ^ 5)`.

## 5. Grammar Error Traps

Syntax errors immediately terminate compiler execution:
- Emits TSV error line: `ERR\tparse\t<line>:<col>: <message>`
- Process exits with standard invocation error code 2.
